From: j7
Subject: j7: copy the panel across every core instead of one

The 900 MHz log puts one present at 8-16 ms, and `copy split build_us=40381
src_us=13686 store_us=330` over seven frames says where it goes: 5.8 ms a frame
building 1920 panel rows from a column-index gather, and 2 ms reading the
client's row.  Every one of those rows is independent - the source surface is
only read, a destination row belongs to exactly one band - and this panel is
1920 of them, so the copy that paces the whole UI was running on one core while
seven sat idle.

Split it: helper threads sleep on a generation counter, take one band each, band
0 is the vsync timer's own, and the timer returns when the last band is done.
Each band gets its own staged source row (srcrow[] was a single static, so a
second thread would have overwritten the row the first was still reading), and
j7fb_bands=<n> picks the band count (default 4, 1 keeps this path off).

This also fixes the row bug 0404 left behind.  0404 wrote the scaled row
straight into the back buffer but kept the duplicate-row skip, and that skip only
made sense when the destination was the panel itself: 1334 logical rows into
1920 panel rows, so 586 panel rows were not written at all and the back buffer
kept presenting whatever it held two frames earlier.  A band now copies those
rows from the row it has just written, which is the same pixel data for
4,320 bytes instead of a second gather.

Expected in the next log: `avg_copy_us` well under the 10-16 ms of the 900 MHz
boot, `build_us` down with it, `copy split` still summing to the present, and no
change in what is on the panel.

Co-Authored-By: dipa-studio <183933817+dipa-studio@users.noreply.github.com>

